$DIR/main.p:22:1: missing return
    sign is declared on line 18 to return int, but the end of its body on line
    22 can be reached without a return statement. Every path through a function
    with results must end in a return, a panic, or a loop that never exits.
    Return a value at the end, for example:
        return 0
    (see "Terminating statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.1)
    (run "pluto explain E0102" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:27:10: cannot use name (variable of type string) as int value in assignment
    count is declared as int on line 25, so it can only hold whole numbers; name
    holds text. Plutonium, like Go, never converts between text and numbers
    automatically.
    To parse a number from text:
        n, err := strconv.Atoi(name)
    (see "Assignability" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.4)
    (run "pluto explain E0023" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:29:9: invalid operation: count + total (mismatched types int and int64)
    count has type int and total has type int64. The operands of + must have the
    same type, and Plutonium, like Go, never converts one to match the other.
    This holds even between number types such as int and int64.
    Convert one operand to the other's type:
        int64(count) + total
    (see "Arithmetic operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.19)
    (run "pluto explain E0046" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:30:10: undefined: cuont
    cuont is not declared in any scope that reaches line 30. Did you mean count
    (declared on line 25)? Names are case-sensitive.
    (see "Declarations and scope" in the Plutonium spec, section 10)
    (run "pluto explain E0075" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:31:23: too many arguments in call to perimeter
	have (number, number)
	want (int)
    perimeter is declared on line 11 with 1 parameter: (side int) int. This call
    passes 2 arguments. Remove the extra arguments, or check that you are
    calling the function you meant.
    (see "Calls" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.14)
    (run "pluto explain E0126" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:32:16: cannot use Square{…} (value of struct type Square) as Shape value in variable declaration: Square does not implement Shape (missing method Area)
    s is declared as Shape, so it can only hold values whose type has every
    method of Shape.
    Shape needs a method Area() int (declared on line 6), and Square has no
    method of that name. A type satisfies an interface by having all its
    methods; nothing else needs to be declared.
    Add the method to Square:
        func (s Square) Area() int {
            ...
        }
    (see "Interface types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.9)
    (run "pluto explain E0021" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:33:7: in call to Zero, cannot infer T (declared at $DIR/main.p:13:11)
    Zero is generic: it has the type parameter list [T], and a call normally
    works the types out from its arguments. T does not appear in the type of any
    argument given here (it may appear only in the results, or in parameters
    left out), so the compiler cannot tell which type to use.
    Give the type arguments in square brackets, with the types you want:
        Zero[T]()
    (see "Type inference" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.17)
    (run "pluto explain E0138" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:34:6: declared and not used: spare
    spare is declared on line 34, but its value is never used. Plutonium, like
    Go, treats this as an error, because an unused variable often means a
    mistake, such as assigning to the wrong variable or forgetting a check.
    Delete the variable, use it, or replace it with _ where it is declared if
    you only need the other values.
    To keep it for now:
        _ = spare
    (see "Variable declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.10)
    (run "pluto explain E0101" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:3:8: "pluto/strings" imported and not used
    This file imports pluto/strings on line 3, but nothing in the file uses
    strings. Plutonium, like Go, treats an unused import as an error, so that
    each file's imports show what it depends on. Delete the import, or use the
    package.
    (see "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3)
    (run "pluto explain E0008" for a longer explanation)
